If the graph of $y=h(x-3)$ is drawn on the same set of axes as the graph above, then the two graphs intersect at one point. What is the sum of the coordinates of that point?

solution:
Let the sum be $S$.  We can split this into 2 geometric series:
\begin{align*}
S &= \left( \frac{1}{7} + \frac{1}{7^2} + \frac{1}{7^3} + \cdots \right) + \left( \frac{1}{7^2} + \frac{1}{7^4} + \frac{1}{7^6} + \cdots \right) \\
  &= \frac{1/7}{1-1/7} + \frac{1/49}{1-1/49} \\
  &= \frac{1/7}{6/7} + \frac{1/49}{48/49} \\
  &= \frac{1}{6} + \frac{1}{48} \\
  &= \boxed{ \frac{3}{16} }
\end{align*}

When using the distributive property, we add the product of $9x$ and $2x^2$ to the product of 4 and $2x^2$:\begin{align*}
(9x+4)\cdot 2x^2 &= 9x\cdot 2x^2+4\cdot 2x^2\\
&= \boxed{18x^3+8x^2}.
\end{align*}

We can simplify directly

\begin{align*}
2\left(\frac{7!}{3!4!}\right) &= 2\left(\frac{7\cdot 6 \cdot 5}{3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1}\right) \\
&= \frac{7 \cdot 6 \cdot 5}{3 \cdot 1} \\
&= 7 \cdot 2 \cdot 5 \\
&= \boxed{70}
\end{align*}

