problem:
Isosceles $\triangle{ABC}$ has a right angle at $C$.  Point $P$ is inside $\triangle{ABC}$, such that $PA=11$, $PB=7$, and $PC=6$. Legs $\overline{AC}$ and $\overline{BC}$ have length $s=\sqrt{a+b\sqrt{2}}$, where $a$ and $b$ are positive integers. What is $a+b$?

[asy]
pair A,B,C,P;
A=(10,0);
B=(0,10);
C=(0,0);
P=(3,3.5);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.7));
draw(A--P,linewidth(0.7));
draw(B--P--C,linewidth(0.7));
label("$A$",A,E);
label("$B$",B,N);
label("$C$",C,S);
label("$P$",P,NE);
label("7",(1.5,6.75),E);
label("6",(1.5, 1.75),E);
label("11",(6.5,1.75),S);
[/asy]

solution:
Rotate $\triangle ABC$ $90^\circ$ counterclockwise about $C$, and let $B^\prime$ and $P^\prime$ be the images of $B$ and $P$, respectively.

[asy]
pair A,B,C,D,P,Q;
A=(10,0);
B=(0,10);
C=(0,0);
D=(-10,0);
P=(2.5,4);
Q=(-4,2.5);
draw(A--B--D--cycle,linewidth(0.7));
draw(B--C,linewidth(0.7));
draw(B--Q--C--P--cycle,linewidth(0.7));
draw(P--Q,linewidth(0.7));
label("$A$",A,S);
label("$B$",B,N);
label("$C$",C,S);
label("$B'$",D,S);
label("$P'$",Q,W);
label("$P$",P,E);
[/asy]

Then $CP^\prime = CP = 6$, and $\angle PCP^\prime =
90^\circ$, so $\triangle PCP^\prime$ is an isosceles right triangle. Thus $PP^\prime = 6\sqrt{2}$, and $BP^\prime = AP = 11$.  Because $\left(6\sqrt{2}\right)^2 + 7^2 = 11^2$, the converse of the Pythagorean Theorem implies that $\angle BPP^\prime = 90^\circ$. Hence $\angle BPC = 135^\circ$.  Applying the Law of Cosines in $\triangle BPC$ gives \[BC^2 = 6^2+7^2-2\cdot 6\cdot 7\cos 135^\circ
= 85+42\sqrt{2},\]and $a+b=\boxed{127}$.

We begin by drawing a diagram and labeling the vertices $A$, $B$, and $C$ as shown:

[asy]
pair A, B, C;
A=(0,0); B=(17,0); C=(5,12);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,E); label("$C$",C,N);
label("$13$",(A+C)/2,NW); label("$12\sqrt{2}$",(B+C)/2,NE); label("$17$",(A+B)/2,S);
[/asy]

We drop a perpendicular from $C$ to $\overline{AB}$ and label the intersection point $X$. $X$ divides $AB$ into two segments $AX$ and $XB$ that sum to 17, so let $AX=x$ and $XB=17-x$.  Let the height of the triangle, $CX$, have length $h$.

[asy]
pair A, B, C, X;
A=(0,0); B=(17,0); C=(5,12); X=(5,0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle); draw(C--X);
label("$A$",A,W); label("$B$",B,E); label("$C$",C,N); label("$X$",X,S);
label("$13$",(A+C)/2,NW); label("$12\sqrt{2}$",(B+C)/2,NE); label("$x$",(A+X)/2,S); label("$17-x$",(B+X)/2,S);
label("$h$",(C+X)/2,E);
draw(rightanglemark(C,X,B,20));
[/asy]

Now we have two right triangles, so we can use the Pythagorean theorem on both triangles to write two equations in terms of $x$ and $h$.  From $\triangle AXC$, we have \[x^2+h^2=13^2,\] and from $\triangle CXB$, we have \[(17-x)^2+h^2=(12\sqrt{2})^2.\] Expanding the second equation gives $289-34x+x^2+h^2=144\cdot 2 = 288$; substituting the first equation into the second gives \[289-34x+13^2=288.\] Simplifying and solving for $x$ yields $1+169=34x$, so $x=170/34=5$.  Plugging this value into the first equation gives \[h^2=13^2-x^2=169-25=144,\] so $h=\sqrt{144}=12$.  Finally, we can compute the area of $\triangle ABC$ to be \[\frac{1}{2}(AB)(h)=\frac{1}{2}(17)(12)=\boxed{102}.\]

