The givens tell us that $\frac{x+y}{2}=18$ and $\sqrt{xy}=\sqrt{92}$, or $x+y=36$ and $xy=92$. $(x+y)^2=x^2+2xy+y^2$, so  \[
x^2+y^2=(x+y)^2-2xy=36^2-2\cdot92=1296-184=\boxed{1112}
\]

Since we know that $f(x)=x^3+2x+1$ and $g(x)=x-1$, we can express $f(g(1))$ as $(x-1)^3+2(x-1)+1$. Thus, when $x=1$ we find \begin{align*} (f(g(1))&=(1-1)^3+2(1-1)+1
\\ &=(0)^3+2(0)+1
\\ &=0+0+1
\\&=\boxed{1}
\end{align*}

